<ProductLayout title="ST-F Pulsed DC Ionizing Bar | 2.5× Faster Neutralizing | Shidike"
  description="ST-F intelligent ionizing bar with the Hibweer L.C.C. system. Neutralizes 2.5 times faster than a conventional bar, ion balance ±100 V, 30–1000 mm working range. For high-speed lines. Made to length from 300 mm."
  image="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.jpg">
  <section class="product-detail-page">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <a href="/products/static-eliminator">Static Eliminator</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span>ST-F Series</span>
      </div>

      <div class="product-top">
        <div class="product-gallery">
          <div class="product-main-image slider-wrapper">
            <button class="slider-arrow left" id="prevImage">‹</button>
            <picture><source srcset="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.webp" type="image/webp" /><img
              id="mainProductImage"
              src="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.jpg"
              alt="ST-F Series Intelligent Ion Bar"
            /></picture>
            <button class="slider-arrow right" id="nextImage">›</button>
          </div>

          <div class="product-thumbs">
            <picture><source srcset="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.webp" type="image/webp" /><img
              class="thumb-image active-thumb"
              src="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.jpg"
              alt="ST-F Series Thumbnail"
              data-full="/assets/img/static-eliminator/st-f.jpg"
            /></picture>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="product-summary">
          <h1>ST-F Intelligent Ionizing Bar</h1>
          <p class="product-short-desc">
            The speed model. Pulsed DC with the Hibweer L.C.C. system, which stops generating negative ions while it makes positive ones and vice versa — neutralizing 2.5 times faster than a conventional bar. Choose it when the material moves too fast for a standard bar to keep up. Ion balance ±100 V, 6.5 kV DC pulse from DC 24 V, 12 W.
          </p>

          <div class="product-actions">
            <button class="quote-btn" type="button">Get a Quote</button>
            <a href="https://wa.me/61436605126" target="_blank" class="whatsapp-btn">WhatsApp</a>
          </div>

          <dl class="key-specs">
            <div><dt>Neutralizing speed</dt><dd><strong>2.5× a conventional bar</strong></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Ion balance</dt><dd><strong>±100 V</strong> — the trade for the speed</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Control</dt><dd><strong>Hibweer L.C.C.</strong> · pulse width varies with frequency</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Working distance</dt><dd><strong>30–1000 mm</strong></dd></div>
            <div><dt>Output</dt><dd><strong>6.5 kV DC pulse</strong> · pulsed DC · DC 24 V, 12 W</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="key-specs-note">The model for lines that move faster than a standard ionizer can follow. Speed is the specification; balance is what you trade for it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>What the ST-F Fixes</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">If any of these is happening on your line, the cause is usually a surface charge nobody is measuring.</p>
        <div class="pd-fix">
          <div class="pd-fix-item">
            <h3>Charge clears at low speed but not at full rate</h3>
            <p>The symptom is intermittent — the line runs clean during setup and starts producing rejects once it is up to speed.</p>
            <span class="sol">2.5× faster neutralizing</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pd-fix-item">
            <h3>Material is only in front of the bar for a fraction of a second</h3>
            <p>On fast indexing or pick-and-place, decay time is the binding constraint, not ion balance.</p>
            <span class="sol">Pulsed DC with phase separation</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pd-fix-item">
            <h3>A bar that worked before a line upgrade stopped working after it</h3>
            <p>Nothing failed — the line simply got faster than the ionizer. The bar is doing exactly what it always did.</p>
            <span class="sol">Swap to the ST-F, same mounting and 24 V supply</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>How It Works</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">Pulsed DC with the Hibweer L.C.C. system: while the bar generates positive ions it stops generating negative ones, and vice versa.</p>
        <figure class="pd-figure wide">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/assets/img/static-eliminator/mode-pulsed-dc.webp" type="image/webp" />
            <img src="/assets/img/static-eliminator/mode-pulsed-dc.jpg"
                 alt="Pulsed DC waveform of the ST-F ionizing bar: a positive phase of high-voltage pulses followed by a negative phase, with pulse width Ton, pulse interval Toff, phase durations T+ and T−, and cycle time T marked"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1672" height="941" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption>
            The positive and negative phases are separated rather than interleaved, and polarity
            never flips inside a pulse. Committing a whole phase to one polarity is what puts
            more of each ion species onto the surface per cycle — and what makes it 2.5× faster.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Separating the two phases in time rather than overlapping them is what produces the speed — neutralization 2.5 times faster than a conventional bar. Where a standard bar needs the material to dwell in front of it, the ST-F clears the charge in the time the material is actually there.</p>
        <p>The Hibweer L.C.C. system also adjusts pulse width according to frequency, so ion generation is matched to the line rather than run flat out.</p>
        <p>Ion balance is ±100 V — the loosest of the three bar series, and the trade you make for the speed. If speed is not your constraint, the ST-G at ±50 V is the better default.</p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Neutralizes twice as fast as a conventional bar, using a supersonic structure that increases air intake</li>
          <li>Up to 100 Hz pulse rate for high-speed moving targets, with 150 % more ion generation than a conventional design</li>
          <li>Hibweer L.C.C. system adjusts pulse width with frequency for flexible control of ion generation</li>
          <li>Detects abnormal discharge, raises an alarm and cuts the high voltage automatically</li>
          <li>Broadband network port for line-level monitoring and industrial networking</li>
          <li>High voltage generated inside the bar — no external power supply box, no fixed HV cable</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Specifications &amp; Dimensions</h2>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tr><td>Model</td><td>ST-F series intelligent ionizing bar</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Input voltage</td><td>DC 24 V</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Power</td><td>12 W</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ion generation</td><td>Corona discharge</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Output</td><td>6.5 kV DC pulse (pulsed DC, Hibweer L.C.C.)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ion balance</td><td>±100 V</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Static decay time</td><td>1 s</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Working distance</td><td>30–1000 mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating temperature</td><td>0–45 °C, no condensation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating humidity</td><td>15–75 % RH, no condensation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cross-section</td><td>30 × 82 mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Length</td><td>Made to order from 300 mm, up to 3 m</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Protection</td><td>Abnormal-discharge alarm with automatic high-voltage cutoff</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Broadband network port</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
        <figure class="pd-figure wide">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/assets/img/static-eliminator/installation-dimensions.webp" type="image/webp" />
            <img src="/assets/img/static-eliminator/installation-dimensions.jpg"
                 alt="ST-F ionizing bar installation dimensions: 82 by 30 mm cross-section, overall view with mounting hole pitch, and mounting bracket detail with an 8 by 20 mm adjustment slot"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption>
            The ST-F shares the bar profile and mounting hardware with the rest of the series:
            a 30 × 82 mm section, two brackets, and an 8 × 20 mm slot so the bar can be shifted
            after the holes are drilled.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Mounting hole pitch by bar length</h3>
        <p>
          Every bar takes two mounting holes, and the pitch is always the bar length less
          120 mm — so you can work out the drilling for any made-to-order length.
        </p>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tr><th>Bar length (L)</th><th>Mounting hole pitch (P)</th><th>Holes</th></tr>
            <tr><td>300 mm</td><td>180 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>600 mm</td><td>480 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>900 mm</td><td>780 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1200 mm</td><td>1080 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1500 mm</td><td>1380 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1800 mm</td><td>1680 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2100 mm</td><td>1980 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2400 mm</td><td>2280 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2700 mm</td><td>2580 mm</td><td>2</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="pd-trust">
          <span>Built in our own Dongguan factory since 2013</span>
          <span>·</span>
          <span>ISO 9001 quality management</span>
          <span>·</span>
          <span>5 granted patents</span>
          <a href="/about">About Shidike →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="pd-mid-cta">
        <p>
          <strong>Not sure the ST-F is the right bar?</strong>
          Send the material, line speed, working distance and measured surface voltage —
          we will come back with a model, a length and a mounting position.
        </p>
        <button class="quote-btn" type="button">Ask an Engineer</button>
      </div>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Where It Goes</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">The bar mounts across the material, facing it, anywhere from 30 mm to 1 m away. Choosing the ST-F for its speed does not cost you mounting flexibility.</p>
        <figure class="pd-figure wide">
          <img src="/assets/img/static-eliminator/install-positions.svg"
               alt="Four typical mounting positions for an ionizing bar on a web line: at the unwind, ahead of the cleaning station, overhead on a gantry, and at the stacker"
               loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1000" height="540" />
          <figcaption>
            Four positions, one line. Which one you need depends on where the charge is created
            rather than where the defect shows up.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="scenario-list">
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>High-speed web and converting lines</h3>
            <p>When line speed rises past what a standard bar can neutralize, the symptom is intermittent — charge cleared at low speed, not at full rate. The ST-F is the model for that.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Fast indexing and pick-and-place</h3>
            <p>Where material is only in front of the bar for a fraction of a second, decay time is the binding constraint, not ion balance.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Retrofitting a line that got faster</h3>
            <p>A bar that worked before a line upgrade often stops working after it. Swapping to the ST-F addresses the speed without changing the mounting or the 24 V supply.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="application-tags">
          <span>Electronics</span>
          <span>Printing</span>
          <span>Optoelectronics</span>
          <span>Textile</span>
          <span>SMT &amp; PCB</span>
          <span>Film &amp; Converting</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Which Model, and What Length</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">
          All three bar series run a 6.5 kV DC pulse from DC 24 V at 12 W over a 30–1000 mm
          working range, and all three are made to length. They differ in how the ions are
          generated, and that decides which one fits your line.
        </p>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tr><td><strong>ST-G</strong> — pulsed AC</td><td>Ion balance <strong>±50 V</strong>, the tightest of the three. Adjustable frequency to 100 Hz. The default choice unless something below applies.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ST-E</strong> — high-frequency pulsed DC</td><td>Ion balance ±80 V, with 150 % higher ion generation. Fixed-polarity pins arranged alternately hold balance across a wide or long span, where an ordinary bar drifts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ST-F</strong> — pulsed DC, Hibweer L.C.C.</td><td>Ion balance ±100 V but <strong>2.5× faster</strong> neutralizing. Choose it when the material moves too fast for a standard bar to keep up.</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3>Made to your width</h3>
        <p>
          Bars are not sold in fixed sizes. We cut the bar to the width of your material,
          anywhere from 300 mm to 3 m, so you are not paying for length you do not use or
          leaving an edge uncovered.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block faq-block pd-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How much faster is the ST-F?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">2.5 times faster than a conventional bar. That comes from the Hibweer L.C.C. system, which stops negative ion generation while producing positive ions and vice versa, rather than overlapping the two phases.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What ion balance does the ST-F achieve?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">±100 V. That is the loosest of the three Shidike bar series — the ST-G is ±50 V and the ST-E is ±80 V — and it is the trade you make for the speed.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">When should I choose the ST-F over the ST-G?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">When the material moves too fast for a standard bar to neutralize in the time it is in front of the bar. If speed is not your constraint, the ST-G has tighter balance and is the better default.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What is the Hibweer L.C.C. system?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">A control system that separates the two ion polarities in time — generating one while suppressing the other — and adjusts pulse width according to frequency. Separating the phases produces the 2.5× speed; varying the pulse width matches output to line speed.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What is the ST-F's working distance?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">30 mm to 1000 mm, the same as the ST-G and ST-E.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Does the ST-F need a separate high-voltage power supply?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">No. High voltage is generated inside the bar from a DC 24 V supply at 12 W — no external power supply box, no fixed-length high-voltage cable.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How long can an ST-F bar be?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Made to order from 300 mm up to 3 m, cut to the width of your material. The cross-section is 30 × 82 mm.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
        <div class="pd-mid-cta" style="margin-top:30px;">
          <p>
            <strong>Request a quote for the ST-F</strong>
            Give us the material, line speed, working distance and measured surface voltage,
            and the length you need.
          </p>
          <button class="quote-btn" type="button">Request a Quote</button>
        </div>
      </section>

